Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Chrome Bends After Midnight
- Story & Context: The debut opens with a four-chord major-7/minor-9 loop played softly on analog polysynth while the drums establish a 92 BPM swing with only kick, rim, shaker, and one clap double every four bars. Aoi states a six-note koto phrase whose final tone is bent upward after the pluck. Kenji answers with a mono synth lead that begins below pitch and glides into the same note before sounding fully. The two gestures become the track's central mirror. Reina places one vibraphone dyad after every second exchange, letting the metal sustain bridge the silence. Marcus enters only in the second half with a breath-heavy low fifth beneath the bass turnaround. The final chorus adds octave koto reinforcement and a more active synth bass but no extra drum layers. The track ends with the synth glide stopping first and the bent koto string continuing to settle acoustically.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Night parking deck with a chrome safety mirror reflecting a koto bridge and analog synth, amber dashboard glow on pale concrete, lane arrows disappearing into dark harbor blue. No conventional lyrics; optional sparse vocoder phrases limited to “after midnight,” “bend it slow,” “chrome remembers,” and “do not rush the turn.”

Track 2: Two Bays From the Vending Light
- Story & Context: Built around a quieter 86 BPM pocket, the second track begins with dry vibraphone and bass flute before any synth appears. Reina plays suspended four-note voicings with the motor off, leaving each chord exposed. Marcus answers with breath-only attacks that gradually acquire pitch. Aoi then introduces clipped low-string koto notes on the offbeats, creating a brittle counter-rhythm against the soft drums. Kenji waits until bar seventeen to introduce bass: a rounded two-note figure that slides only at the end of every fourth phrase. Midway through, the arrangement drops to koto, rim click, and the electrical hum of the parking structure. When the full groove returns, the harmony remains unchanged but the vibraphone moves an octave higher, making the same progression feel illuminated. The closing bars leave only one vending-machine-like synth chord, a low flute exhale, and two koto harmonics.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty upper parking deck with one illuminated vending machine two bays away from a koto and vibraphone setup, blue-white fluorescent ceiling light mixed with warm amber machine glow, bass flute resting against a concrete wheel stop. Optional whispered or talkbox fragments limited to “two bays over,” “leave the light on,” “same chord, higher glass,” and “breathe before the bass.”

Track 3: Suspension Settles at Harbor Exit
- Story & Context: The EP closer uses the most active bass line but the sparsest melody. Kenji programs a 96 BPM pocket with slightly delayed snare, rolling mono bass, and tiny shaker ghosts. Aoi avoids full phrases for the first minute, contributing isolated plucks and muted string knocks while Reina sustains long upper extensions. Marcus plays low chromatic sighs that rise into the tonic only at section endings. The central section introduces a descending koto line whose bent final notes gradually shorten across four repetitions, while the synth lead does the opposite: each portamento becomes longer. The opposing envelope creates the feeling of one suspension system compressing while another extends. In the last sixteen bars, all melody disappears and the groove reduces to bass, drums, vibraphone motor noise, and harbor ambience. Aoi closes the EP with one low koto note pressed downward until its pitch settles beneath the final synth chord.
